{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2506 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "794b0eac5f0ffc526e6f84e4de03531ffa71fe3f8a4876a3106824b7e462ee8f" :edb/db-before-t 2505 :edb/db-after-t 2506 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535818 50 #inst "2026-10-09T08:04:41.478Z" 13194139535818 true] [17592186175761 1100 "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535818 false] [17592186175761 1100 "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/assets/1a6c3918b93419f9576951374ebd69b2df271106345493084ffa0797b0a35847.png)" 13194139535818 true] [17592186175761 1145 "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA = PJP^{-1}\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A$, and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J$, we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving\n\n$$\n\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}5 - λ & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(5 - λ)(-2 - λ) - 4 \\cdot 2 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} - 3λ - 18 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 6)(λ + 3) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=-3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=6\n$$\n\nSince we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable, and a Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n**Note**: A Jordan canonical form is unique up to permutations of its Jordan blocks. Therefore, another Jordan canonical form for this matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139535818 false] [17592186175761 1145 "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA = PJP^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda )-4\\cdot 2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -18 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -6)(\\lambda +3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=-3$ and $\\lambda_2=6.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable, and a Jordan canonical form is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/assets/d6c87739f52f450b27c68a118bba04e51e68a377da7d0a16ae0658e1b2d547f0.png)\n\n**Note**: A Jordan canonical form is unique up to permutations of its Jordan blocks. Therefore, another Jordan canonical form for this matrix is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/assets/7bd509d943bf0707e75c9436aee3a511f8366502bbf8121b715cec8ce366953b.png)" 13194139535818 true] [17592186175783 1100 "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535818 false] [17592186175783 1100 "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/assets/69fcceae9cb181cf79e1b12f0db37ed2ba27841aaebb089ffa24d0d365b9db56.png)" 13194139535818 true] [17592186175783 1145 "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA = PJP^{-1}\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A$, and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J$, we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving\n\n$$\n\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 - λ & 3 \\\\ -3 & -4 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(2 - λ)(-4 - λ) - 3 \\cdot (-3) &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} + 2λ + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 1)(λ + 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=-1\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139535818 false] [17592186175783 1145 "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA = PJP^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ -3 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )-3\\cdot (-3) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=-1.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/assets/c8716d15efb054f488535b139e201dda7bc61a3fb8880caf50d65bee515c2cc1.png)" 13194139535818 true] [17592186175784 1100 "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535818 false] [17592186175784 1100 "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/assets/4b66afe6c1771afad4987215e3ddc02865a9f873ae93050f940c36bc3ff86356.png)" 13194139535818 true] [17592186175784 1145 "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA = PJP^{-1}\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A$, and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nNotice that $A$ is a scalar matrix, which means that it is already in Jordan canonical form. Therefore, it has a single (repeated) eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda = -3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139535818 false] [17592186175784 1145 "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA = PJP^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nNotice that $A$ is a scalar matrix, which means that it is already in Jordan canonical form. Therefore, it has a single (repeated) eigenvalue $\\lambda = -3,$ and\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/assets/2be33154f6a1933a12a3c43474464edb9c4e3fd2b2a4213d789b49fc1e4aec77.png)" 13194139535818 true] [17592186175913 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368099 13194139535818 true] [17592186175913 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368106 13194139535818 true] [17592186175913 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368154 13194139535818 true] [17592186175933 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368078 13194139535818 true] [17592186175933 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368085 13194139535818 true] [17592186175933 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368092 13194139535818 true] [17592186175933 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368161 13194139535818 true] [17592186175933 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368168 13194139535818 true] [17592186175934 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368113 13194139535818 true] [17592186175934 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368120 13194139535818 true] [17592186175934 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368127 13194139535818 true] [17592186175934 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368134 13194139535818 true] [17592186175934 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368141 13194139535818 true] [17592186176147 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535818 true] [17592186176147 1100 "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 5 \\\\ -10 & -9\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139535818 false] [17592186176147 1100 "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 5 \\\\ -10 & -9\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535818 true] [17592186176147 1145 "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}6-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -10 & -9-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (6-\\lambda )(-9-\\lambda )-(5)(-10) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable, and a Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{1}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-4)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535818 true] [17592186176204 1017 #edb/ref 17592186173886 13194139535818 false] [17592186176204 1017 #edb/ref 17592186368148 13194139535818 true] [17592186176204 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535818 true] [17592186176204 1100 "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139535818 false] [17592186176204 1100 "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535818 true] [17592186176204 1145 "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 1 & 8-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(8-\\lambda )-(-2)(1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +42 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -7)(\\lambda -6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=7$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable, and a Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{1}(7)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(6)=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535818 true] [1759218